#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064146 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c24da7ef-0070-5aae-bade-c82ea56476ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9845" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = x^5 (\\cos x)^x.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\ny'(x) = x^4 (\\cos x)^x \\left( 5 + g(x) \\right).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = x^5 (\\cos x)^x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{5}{x}+(x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{5}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{5}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{5}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = x^5 (\\cos x)^x {:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}(\\frac{5}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}(5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = x \\ln ( \\cos  x ) - x^2 \\tan x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064147 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"}] :question/id #uuid "7db11c8c-344b-5d87-a8ae-3b006e5bd92b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9846" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{g(x)(x+3)^2}{(x+5)^7}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+5)^{6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+5)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y =  \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}(\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}(\\frac{3(x+5)-6(x+3)}{(x+3)(x+5)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}(\\frac{-3x-3}{(x+3)(x+5)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3(x+1)(x+3)^{2}}{(x+5)^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{3(x+1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064148 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "e0cec84d-96cf-5fed-ba05-a5cfc57841a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9847" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{x^2}{x^5+ 1}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\ny' =  \\dfrac{g(x)}{3\\sqrt[3]{x(x^5+1)^4}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3}}{({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2/3}}{({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2/3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}+1}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y =  \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{x^2}{x^5+ 1}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{2({x}^{5}+1)-5{x}^{5}}{x({x}^{5}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{x({x}^{5}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}({x}^{5}+1)^{3}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x({x}^{5}+1)^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-3x^5+2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cefdade-e796-5084-a7a3-e6383c60ef43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506042} {:answer/id #uuid "629e95fd-c8c0-5fb3-a0d4-61fa413fa7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506080} {:answer/id #uuid "698dcec7-e3e1-5e7e-94be-301f09412429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506091} {:answer/id #uuid "9e897ff6-74eb-563e-ad6f-9b136723fdd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506193} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bd5208-208d-5b00-a104-e894b45eded1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cefdade-e796-5084-a7a3-e6383c60ef43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506042} :answer-field/id #uuid "dde27e2d-4a7c-5959-b482-0d3df3d32fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "174736f7-0588-55bb-bfa7-155dc6d18a73" :question/math-academy-id "q-138800" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={3}^{x+4}\\cdot (a{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}+c).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+1}\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x+4}(4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)({x}^{4}+1)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x+4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3){x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)=4+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24dcf1bc-40b1-5496-a498-3a7dae4ffe85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "271" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505972} {:answer/id #uuid "94c691dd-0578-5e12-9cc1-90a7a7bb4085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506165} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f872ba-2453-55d3-97e1-d1431eaa6668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506204} {:answer/id #uuid "e2265b1b-5fa7-5e22-a840-d2ec09a5ad1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506326} {:answer/id #uuid "eef1c07b-39fe-56ff-ad05-22dd4b396724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef1c07b-39fe-56ff-ad05-22dd4b396724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506349} :answer-field/id #uuid "13bb00dc-f20f-5ab2-8e37-6185dcb84a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adde1803-10ec-5c3d-8c04-d7e2c5d99587" :question/math-academy-id "q-138830" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(ax+63-8({x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{8}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6(x+8)+5(x+3)-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(x+3)(x+8)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6x+48+5x+15-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=11+11+24=46.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39eac370-b597-5b13-a139-ed248af8a490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506009} {:answer/id #uuid "3e37cd6e-52b0-57de-acb7-d9a7a4ef0fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506014} {:answer/id #uuid "6c234f23-48fe-556c-a0b1-24c477bfbdfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506100} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bacdde-7997-50ac-944f-7a50e4a49e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506288} {:answer/id #uuid "deac3608-20ae-52c0-97b0-276c7fcb4d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2bacdde-7997-50ac-944f-7a50e4a49e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506288} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3dff71e-7253-5db5-a8db-526f8787e942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11c48698-c6fd-5725-a4b0-1737e6fdc309" :question/math-academy-id "q-138831" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((ax+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f59a9ba-364e-508e-a41a-55a558b2bb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505941} {:answer/id #uuid "478822c2-e030-5e90-884b-19998c062866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506029} {:answer/id #uuid "75b16ee9-4f9f-5d41-b9b6-01fc0fada42c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506121} {:answer/id #uuid "9e556be2-fc3f-5682-8727-573dea82908b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506192} {:answer/id #uuid "cb83d552-a42d-5c11-aa25-bd36f59bc3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f59a9ba-364e-508e-a41a-55a558b2bb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505941} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b209a8-1c66-5330-a9ec-0e55e08cba8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbe4c4ed-7bf7-5234-8564-800989eb5aa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138839" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}((x+2)^{2}+g(x)).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+1)^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-4x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-(2-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2+4x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+1}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(\\frac{2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)}{x+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4x+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+(x+2)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c28e5d-0de9-502d-81aa-7351f4920069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505924} {:answer/id #uuid "90c27865-1c0b-519b-81bb-de523a0ce9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506157} {:answer/id #uuid "93851dca-79be-57a5-b496-18ef7187487f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506161} {:answer/id #uuid "b193db5a-f715-5820-8744-ed3301ee43c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506234} {:answer/id #uuid "ebacda43-d38a-518b-8124-0083b7f84186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebacda43-d38a-518b-8124-0083b7f84186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506342} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d436c14-aa02-5c1c-8f96-e30ae5edad2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76085196-6a2f-5c76-90cb-5c742aa98a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138849" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x-3)^{3}(a{x}^{2}+bx+c).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{8/2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x(x-3)+4({x}^{2}+1)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{1/2}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=7+(-9)+4=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b15c20-ae96-5510-bdf6-238327aebb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505977} {:answer/id #uuid "9420b75f-9ddc-56f2-8501-2ed81e404fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506163} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa83dc4-ef7c-5fb9-9ca5-7ad2f609a832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506198} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa37242-7820-582d-b32a-092b97e43ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506282} {:answer/id #uuid "f9347296-ee2c-5eec-91b9-fff04cdeed07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27b15c20-ae96-5510-bdf6-238327aebb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505977} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b45ba0-9d41-52b1-8d31-cba0ed77bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06abf62c-6dfa-52f6-818c-3858ac967b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-138850" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((ax+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(b{x}^{2}+cx)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab36fdc-7bd5-5430-8835-a0815a440065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505956} {:answer/id #uuid "6d88a707-e10c-5afc-bea5-52cb9dd63b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506103} {:answer/id #uuid "7e987b10-51ae-5db1-b678-922798639d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506131} {:answer/id #uuid "9515e64c-16bf-58d0-b027-ae01ea34515a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506166} {:answer/id #uuid "d0164b4c-0fc5-5386-8a13-5df40b0b0015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0164b4c-0fc5-5386-8a13-5df40b0b0015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506284} :answer-field/id #uuid "de6a18ff-6676-56f5-b05e-7d4da313f1e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc8a78e5-ffc8-5ad6-abfc-11fb82c73f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-138865" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{a{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+c}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{3/2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2x}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2)-(x+1)({x}^{4}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+2x+2-({x}^{5}+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{3/2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+4+2=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320e3fd9-f5be-5ba8-b2a9-0494b08fbfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320e3fd9-f5be-5ba8-b2a9-0494b08fbfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506000} :answer-field/id #uuid "81cfe4de-fb10-510e-9446-d496d731b1e0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03563df1-ba6d-5b1c-a0b5-5ad046bfe9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03563df1-ba6d-5b1c-a0b5-5ad046bfe9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505919} :answer-field/id #uuid "e218e9f7-9f97-55c2-8470-2d5b2b9d85fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64e1cc7c-fdf8-54e9-af16-e127bdf5e7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64e1cc7c-fdf8-54e9-af16-e127bdf5e7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506082} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5316a49-e71e-5b2a-be34-e3d00be0b5c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62910e77-69f8-56ea-ac31-ee2dbfe3c8ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-312655" :question/problem "Given that $y=({x}^{3}-2x){e}^{{x}^{4}+5x},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}={e}^{{x}^{4}+5x}($\n{{field-1}}${x}^{6}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{4}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-10x-2)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{3}-2x){e}^{{x}^{4}+5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{3}-2x){e}^{{x}^{4}+5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}-2x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{4}+5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}-2x)+{x}^{4}+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}-2x)+{x}^{4}+5x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{x}^{3}-2x}+4{x}^{3}+5 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{x}^{3}-2x}+4{x}^{3}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{x}^{3}-2x}+4{x}^{3}+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{3}-2x){e}^{{x}^{4}+5x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{3}-2x){e}^{{x}^{4}+5x}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{x}^{3}-2x}+4{x}^{3}+5) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{4}+5x}[3{x}^{2}-2+(4{x}^{3}+5)({x}^{3}-2x)] \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{4}+5x}(4{x}^{6}-8{x}^{4}+5{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-10x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4721e6-a280-5e36-9c3e-3d0197e080fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan(x)\\ln(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4721e6-a280-5e36-9c3e-3d0197e080fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan(x)\\ln(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505940} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bed9cfc-3a69-5d51-ba57-741bfaabbf0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61a2565a-e401-5260-aae7-f12b167eb293" :question/math-academy-id "q-312658" :question/problem "Given that $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}={x}^{x}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+{sec}^{2}⁡(x))$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}+{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419792fe-6389-5363-a771-125381c66945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x^3+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "419792fe-6389-5363-a771-125381c66945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x^3+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506021} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cca9dd-6ad8-57a8-a947-88e8f34aa52d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "254041d3-aef5-5074-80f4-67b8b1344bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-312672" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}+4x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8>0\\text{ }∀x\\in R$, so $|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}[\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot ({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(8{x}^{3}+8x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102790ff-7839-5d1c-a7c1-c89c090c0d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "102790ff-7839-5d1c-a7c1-c89c090c0d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505942} :answer-field/id #uuid "274547bb-be34-5b65-bb9d-f32876fabd37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4323142-e936-5cd3-b6de-3092378faa67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4323142-e936-5cd3-b6de-3092378faa67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506359} :answer-field/id #uuid "94665d5f-2175-540b-accc-e742e344e1b0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5274db64-5e9a-58d1-a778-b4f7735c7525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5274db64-5e9a-58d1-a778-b4f7735c7525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506050} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03d9a12-bbbf-5ee1-a420-92022427414d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a442047-ed6e-5bb4-89b9-beaee4b1e553" :question/math-academy-id "q-338291" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}.$  Find the derivative of $y.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | $(x+2)^{3}\\,(-5{x}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{2}\\,+$ \n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+2)^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{3(3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{3}+2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4({x}^{3}+2x-1)-(x+2)(9{x}^{2}+6)}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{3}(-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16)}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7fc885f-4e1a-55a5-802c-ac89deca17cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7fc885f-4e1a-55a5-802c-ac89deca17cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506298} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b0091fa-b5a4-5939-b660-79b995ca2d23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506416} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf1bee4f-faf3-5c66-a6e9-0247b5788e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf1bee4f-faf3-5c66-a6e9-0247b5788e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506280} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c634e48-9a53-537b-8310-97d5118fb24d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7e7a9b3-3d4f-54b8-8563-cde99f24e6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7e7a9b3-3d4f-54b8-8563-cde99f24e6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506244} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1aa8109-3dc2-5c64-ba41-e3f530fba656" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9ab7545-6c81-5c20-9f25-5d780715f99d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343327" :question/problem "Given that $y={(2x-3)}^{7}{({x}^{3}+5)}^{4},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}={(2x-3)}^{6}{({x}^{3}+5)}^{3}($\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={(2x-3)}^{7}{({x}^{3}+5)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(2x-3)}^{7}{({x}^{3}+5)}^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(2x-3)}^{7})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({({x}^{3}+5)}^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-3)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-3)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+5)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =7\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x-3}+4\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+5} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =7\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x-3}+4\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(7\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x-3}+4\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(2x-3)}^{7}{({x}^{3}+5)}^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(2x-3)}^{7}{({x}^{3}+5)}^{4}(7\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x-3}+4\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+5}) \\\\  & ={(2x-3)}^{6}{({x}^{3}+5)}^{3}[14({x}^{3}+5)+12{x}^{2}(2x-3)] \\\\  & ={(2x-3)}^{6}{({x}^{3}+5)}^{3}(38{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}+70)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ebc9a01-e27f-509b-a68a-42b56dc2ad7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ebc9a01-e27f-509b-a68a-42b56dc2ad7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506045} :answer-field/id #uuid "7487540a-070b-5d24-9ba2-dc636972e043" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506432} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec54cd01-acca-5a25-833a-60eab750baf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec54cd01-acca-5a25-833a-60eab750baf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506345} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0efc8ad-b4c8-5f06-a724-3416448817ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97af42c5-8f26-50fc-a29e-fb1a80f1a04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97af42c5-8f26-50fc-a29e-fb1a80f1a04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506175} :answer-field/id #uuid "d150bf24-0895-5aa8-98a7-c4a97ebe264b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "310e9298-b624-54d9-ac03-bed77b7fdecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343350" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.$  Find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | ${6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,($\n{{field-1}}$(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient, product, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3-x)^{3})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}-3\\cdot \\frac{-1}{3-x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4(3-x)+3(x-2)}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18)}{(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237677c3-f4bf-5920-adb2-829ac28f98fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x^2+1)\\ln(\\sin x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "237677c3-f4bf-5920-adb2-829ac28f98fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x^2+1)\\ln(\\sin x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505968} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f1e7c1d-bc39-5ec5-a66f-51aa65b8b878" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d911ae7-875c-567e-a470-7c373f982af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343542" :question/problem "Given that $y=({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}($\n{{field-1}}$\\,+\\,2x+x({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (2x)+(x)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}(2x+({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}(({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2x+x({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1424a6ad-ac38-52fa-bea7-f95d3db15e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14x^2+63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1424a6ad-ac38-52fa-bea7-f95d3db15e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14x^2+63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505950} :answer-field/id #uuid "61e10619-0457-5f47-8c3c-d968af092244" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19b1b664-4a0c-5ebc-8d57-f886d4c9f0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-343952" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}=$ | $-$ | $x($\n{{field-1}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}]}^{1/4} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot (2x)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x}{2({x}^{2}+4)}-\\frac{8x}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x(2{x}^{2}+1)-16x({x}^{2}+4)}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{-14{x}^{3}-63x}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x(14{x}^{2}+63)}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
